===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|iniquities}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[deviation|Deviation]] from what is [[right#Adjective|right]]; [[gross#Adjective|gross]] [[injustice]], [[sin#Noun|sin]], [[wickedness]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello Q1|act=IV|scene=i|page=66|passage=If you be ſo fond ouer her '''iniquity''', giue her patent to offend, for if it touches not you, it comes neere no body.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Job|15|15|16|url=https://archive.org/stream/Bible1611/Binder1#page/n627/mode/1up|passage=Beholde, he putteth no truſt in his Saints, yea, the heauens are not cleane in his ſight. How much more abominable and filthie is man, which drinketh '''iniquitie''' like water?}}
#* {{quote-av|en|actor=[[w:Samuel L. Jackson|Samuel L[eroy] Jackson]]|role=Jules Winnfield|writer={{w|Quentin Tarantino}}|title={{w|Pulp Fiction}}|date=21 May 1994|passage=The path of the righteous man is beset on all sides by the '''iniquities''' of the selfish and the tyranny of evil men.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Oliver Brown|title={{w|Oscar Pistorius}} jailed for five years – sport afforded no protection against his tragic fallibilities: Bladerunner’s punishment for killing {{w|Reeva Steenkamp}} is but a frippery when set against the burden that her bereft parents, June and Barry, must carry [print version: No room for sentimentality in this tragedy, 13 September 2014, page&nbsp;S22]|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20191009115500/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/worldnews/oscar-pistorius/11089621/Oscar-Pistorius-jailed-for-five-years-sport-afforded-no-protection-against-his-tragic-fallibilities.html|archivedate=9 October 2019|editor={{w|Jason Seiken}}|newspaper={{w|The Daily Telegraph}} (Sport)|location=London|publisher={{w|Telegraph Media Group}}|date=21 October 2014|issn=0307-1235|oclc=635239717|passage=But ever since the concept of "hamartia" recurred through {{w|Aristotle}}'s ''[[w:Poetics (Aristotle)|Poetics]]'', in an attempt to describe man's ingrained '''iniquity''', our impulse has been to identify a telling defect in those brought suddenly and dramatically low.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} An [[act#Noun|act]] of [[great#Adjective|great]] [[injustice]] or [[unfairness]]; a [[sinful]] or [[wicked#Adjective|wicked]] act; an [[unconscionable]] [[deed#Noun|deed]].

